## 2. Scope split — DOM owns form/control/layout; CanvasKit owns rich graphics, deferred

### [CXC-DOM-SCOPE-SPLIT] MUST realize form/control/layout as native DOM elements; MUST NOT redraw an interactive control on a raster `<canvas>` element; rich-graphics backend is a SEPARATE unit, deferred

- requirement: (MUST, testable) every widget this backend classifies as a CONTROL — a widget whose
  Python/Skia realization is interactive and whose semantics map onto a native HTML form control or an
  accessible interactive element (button, text field, checkbox, select, textarea, date/time picker,
  table, tree) — MUST be realized as a real DOM element of the matching semantic kind (`<button>`,
  `<input>`, `<select>`, `<table>`, an ARIA `role="tree"` structure, etc.), never as pixels painted
  inside an HTML5 `<canvas>` element. A build that renders a styled `<div>` pretending to be a button,
  reachable only by mouse and invisible to a screen reader, fails this requirement even if it is
  pixel-identical to the Skia rendering.
- requirement: (MUST, testable) rich-graphics widgets (gauges, data visualizations, the colonial/BSG
  UX) are OUT OF SCOPE for this backend and MUST NOT be attempted here. Their browser realization is a
  SEPARATE backend built on CanvasKit — Skia compiled to WASM, the SAME rendering library the Python
  Skia backend already uses, so pixel behavior can stay close to the reference tier — scoped to the WEB
  path only, and explicitly DEFERRED, not designed by this spec. MOBILE rich-graphics realization is
  DEFERRED ENTIRELY: no RN-Skia (or any other mobile graphics backend) commitment exists at this time.
- canvaskit-verification-caveat: (david-ruling, 2026-08-25, restored per the DOM-backend audit's Q1,
  [DEC-DOM-AUDIT-RESOLVED-2026-08-25]) CanvasKit is the CHOSEN, NOT YET VERIFIED, deferred web graphics
  backend — pending the render-engineer's live-doc verification of current RN-Skia-web maturity and
  CanvasKit build options before the deferred unit is actually built. This spec MUST NOT be read as
  claiming that verification has happened; the deferred unit remains unbuilt and unverified.
- why-not-react-native-skia-as-the-base: (architect-recommendation — the flavor choice, framed by
  David's 2026-08-25 realization-declares-envelope ruling, [DX-REALIZATION-DECLARES-ENVELOPE]) React
  Native Skia is CanvasKit PLUS a React component/state paradigm layered on top; adopting it AS THE BASE
  graphics library would fork this platform's own imperative `Canvas`-contract paradigm
  ([CXC-R-DRAW-CONTRACT]) into a second, incompatible one for one backend only, and — per the
  realization-declares-envelope principle — would silently declare every widget built against it
  platform-bound even where it never needed to be. Plain CanvasKit (when that unit is eventually built)
  keeps the imperative draw-call contract intact and portable across every backend, ESP32 included.
  RN-Skia is NOT rejected outright: it remains a legitimate PER-WIDGET escape hatch for a widget that is
  INHERENTLY platform-bound (a fullscreen complex-graphics widget an ESP32-S3 could never run regardless)
  — never the default and never the base library.
- why-not-redraw-controls: David's own framing (the Flutter-scale trap): a raster-only browser UI
  reinvents keyboard navigation, screen-reader semantics, IME/i18n text input, and platform form
  affordances that the browser already provides for free — the exact tax Flutter/Skia-everywhere UIs
  pay on the web. Leveraging real `<input>`/`<button>`/`<select>` elements is not a shortcut; it is the
  only way this backend does not have to re-implement accessibility from scratch.
- verify-machine: an automated accessibility audit (axe-core or equivalent) run against a rendered DOM
  scene containing every v1 control type ([CXC-DOM-CONTROL-SET-V1]) reports zero missing
  name/role/value violations on those elements; a DOM snapshot of a scene containing a gauge widget
  contains NO gauge-specific markup from this backend (the gauge is either absent from a DOM-only scene
  or rendered by the separate, not-yet-built CanvasKit backend — never faked in DOM).
- verify-human: a screen-reader pass (VoiceOver or NVDA) over a rendered config form confirms every
  control is reachable by Tab, announces its label and current value, and can be operated without a
  mouse — the concrete proof that "leverage HTML" bought what it was chosen for.
- status: proposed
- provenance: david-ruling — THREE-WAY calibrated per [DEC-DOM-AUDIT-RESOLVED-2026-08-25] (the audit
  that found this node's original single `david-ruling` tag over-attributed the CanvasKit flavor choice
  to David's verbatim word — see `docs/composeserver/AUDIT-dom-backend.md` finding INVENTED-1). (1)
  david-ruling — the DOM-vs-canvas SCOPE SPLIT itself, the node's primary assertion: Fork A of
  [DEC-COMPOSEUX-DOM-BACKEND-DESIGN-2026-08-25], "v1 DOM backend renders the form/control/layout subset
  as NATIVE DOM elements (leverage HTML — free accessibility/keyboard/i18n; do NOT redraw controls in
  canvas)... rich graphics... = a SEPARATE canvas-in-browser backend, DEFERRED," ratified by David's
  "Good resolution for A" (line 26396). The `why-not-redraw-controls` framing restates David's own
  Flutter-scale-trap reasoning as recorded in the
  ratified decision. (2) david-ruling — the CANVASKIT SELECTION for the deferred WEB path, mobile
  deferred entirely: David's own 2026-08-25 ruling ([DEC-DOM-AUDIT-RESOLVED-2026-08-25], resolving the
  audit's Q1). In the ORIGINAL 2026-08-24 design session David only ASKED "RN-Skia or CanvasKit?" (line
  26374) and this node wrongly presented the architect's recommended answer as David's own word; David
  has now RULED it directly, restoring the architect's original "pending live-doc verification" caveat
  (line 26382) rather than presenting CanvasKit as a verified commitment. (3) architect-recommendation —
  the RN-Skia-as-per-widget-escape-hatch framing in `why-not-react-native-skia-as-the-base`: grounded in
  David's 2026-08-25 [DX-REALIZATION-DECLARES-ENVELOPE] principle, but the specific application to
  RN-Skia's role here is the architect's synthesis, not David's verbatim word.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DOM]
- see-also: [CXC-R-DRAW-CONTRACT], [DX-NARROW-WAIST], [CXC-VISION-SEMANTIC-REALIZATION], [DX-REALIZATION-DECLARES-ENVELOPE]

