## 3. Hybrid composition — one page, two backends, no coordination between them

### [CXC-DOM-HYBRID-COMPOSITION] MUST allow a single scene to compose DOM chrome and canvas islands; neither backend needs to be aware of the other

- requirement: (MUST, testable) a `.compose.yaml` scene MAY declare widgets realized by this DOM
  backend alongside widgets realized by the (deferred, future) CanvasKit backend in the same document.
  This backend's own realization scope is bounded to the widget classes it declares support for
  ([CXC-DOM-CONTROL-SET-V1] plus the generic primitive-mapped layout/chrome path,
  [CXC-DOM-CANVAS-SUBCLASS]); any widget outside that scope MUST NOT be silently dropped — it is either
  realized by a canvas island mounted at the widget's declared rect (once that backend exists) or, on a
  DOM-only build with no CanvasKit island available yet, rendered as a NAMED placeholder (the widget id
  and type, visibly marked "not yet realized on this backend") rather than left blank with no signal.
- why-no-coordination-needed: each backend independently subscribes exactly the namespaces its own
  widgets bind ([CXC-DOM-TWO-WAY-BINDING]) and independently publishes on its own controls' events
  ([CXC-DOM-CONTROL-PUBLISH-REUSE]) — the semantic namespace is the ONLY contract between them
  ([CXC-VISION-SEMANTIC-REALIZATION]), so composing the two backends on one page requires no shared
  runtime state, no shared event bus beyond the ComposeBus connection itself, and no negotiation.
- negative-control: a scene containing ONLY DOM-realizable widgets renders with zero references to
  CanvasKit/WASM anywhere in the page's network requests — this backend never pulls in the graphics
  backend's payload for a page that does not need it.
- verify-machine: a mixed-scene fixture (once CanvasKit exists) renders both a DOM control and a canvas
  island with the DOM control's publish and the canvas island's redraw both independently observable in
  a test harness; a DOM-only fixture's network trace contains no CanvasKit/WASM asset request.
- verify-human: none required — this node's failure surface (a missing widget rendering as a named
  placeholder rather than silent blank space) is machine-observable via a DOM query for the placeholder
  marker.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis, realizing Fork A's "a page COMPOSES both (hybrid DOM chrome + canvas
  islands)" as a queryable node.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DOM]
- see-also: [CXC-DOM-SCOPE-SPLIT], [CXC-VISION-SEMANTIC-REALIZATION]

---

## 4. Realization is per-widget-class; a control's canvas realization is built lazily

### [CXC-DOM-REALIZATION-SPLIT] MUST dispatch each widget type to exactly one of two realization paths: a bespoke native-element renderer (controls) or the generic primitive-mapped `Canvas` subclass (layout/chrome); MUST NOT build a Skia/CanvasKit realization for an admin control that never needs it

- requirement: (MUST, testable) this backend MUST maintain a CONTROL RENDERER REGISTRY — a mapping from
  widget `type` string to a dedicated JS function that constructs the semantic native DOM element(s) for
  that widget type directly (never through `fill_rect`/`draw_text`/etc.) — covering every widget type in
  [CXC-DOM-CONTROL-SET-V1] plus every existing interactive widget type this backend re-realizes
  ([CXC-DOM-CONTROL-PUBLISH-REUSE]: `emit_button`, `text_input`, `radio_button_group`, `theme_toggle`,
  and any other widget whose Python/Skia counterpart is interactive).
- requirement: (MUST, testable) any widget type NOT present in the control renderer registry — pure
  layout containers, static backgrounds, decorative shapes, non-interactive labels — MUST fall through
  to the generic DOM `Canvas` ABC subclass ([CXC-DOM-CANVAS-SUBCLASS]), which renders it exactly as the
  widget's own `draw()` method already draws it on every other backend (Skia, SVG), via the SAME
  Canvas-contract draw calls, translated to DOM elements. This is what keeps [DX-NARROW-WAIST] honored:
  a widget that has not been given a bespoke DOM realization still renders correctly, just without the
  native-accessibility upgrade a control gets.
- requirement: (MUST, testable) a widget's CanvasKit (Skia-in-browser) realization MUST NOT be built,
  loaded, or referenced by this backend for any widget type that is fully served by the control renderer
  registry or the generic Canvas-primitive path. An admin/config control (the primary v1 target,
  [CXC-DOM-CONTROL-SET-V1]) never needs the canvas/embedded tier and therefore never triggers it — the
  laziness is structural (the registry dispatch never reaches for it), not merely a performance choice
  deferred to runtime profiling.
- negative-control: rendering a scene composed entirely of [CXC-DOM-CONTROL-SET-V1] widgets produces
  zero CanvasKit/WASM network requests and zero calls into any Skia-in-browser code path — a test
  asserts the absence, not merely that the result "looks right".
- verify-machine: a widget type present in the control renderer registry dispatches to its registered
  function (spy-asserted call), never falls through to the generic Canvas-primitive path; a widget type
  absent from the registry dispatches to the generic path and produces DOM elements matching its
  `draw()` calls' primitive sequence; no CanvasKit asset is requested for a control-only scene.
- verify-human: none required — this node's failure surface (wrong dispatch path, or an unwanted lazy-
  load firing) is fully machine-observable via network trace and call-spy assertions.
- status: proposed
- provenance: david-ruling — Fork A: "realization is per-widget-class... a control's Skia realization is
  built LAZILY only if it ever needs the canvas/embedded tier (admin controls won't)." The registry
  mechanism and the "structural, not merely deferred" framing are architect-synthesis, the implementation
  answer this spec had to invent to make the ruling testable.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DOM]
- see-also: [CXC-DOM-CANVAS-SUBCLASS], [CXC-DOM-CONTROL-PUBLISH-REUSE], [DX-NARROW-WAIST]

