## 5. The DOM `Canvas` subclass — primitives to elements (greenfield)

### [CXC-DOM-CANVAS-SUBCLASS] MUST implement every abstract method of the `Canvas` ABC as a DOM-element-producing operation, backed by an explicit style/transform stack (not a painted raster)

- requirement: (MUST, testable) a new `Canvas` subclass — the fallback realization for layout/chrome
  widgets ([CXC-DOM-REALIZATION-SPLIT]) — MUST implement all 35 `@abstractmethod` members of
  `python/composeio/render/canvas.py`'s `Canvas` ABC (measured 2026-08-25: `fill_rect`, `stroke_rect`,
  `fill_round_rect`, `stroke_round_rect`, `fill_circle`, `stroke_circle`, `fill_arc`, `stroke_arc`,
  `draw_line`, `draw_path`, `fill_path`, `set_fill_color`, `set_stroke_color`, `set_stroke_width`,
  `set_linear_gradient`, `set_radial_gradient`, `set_shadow`, `clear_shadow`, `set_opacity`, `save`,
  `restore`, `translate`, `rotate`, `scale`, `clip_rect`, `clip_round_rect`, `set_font`, `draw_text`,
  `measure_text`, `draw_image`, `load_image`, `decode_image`, `create_surface`, `present`,
  `get_surface_size`) plus MAY override the two concrete-default methods (`set_font_scale`,
  `present_partial`) exactly as any other backend may. This is the SAME contract the Skia and SVG
  backends already implement — no widget author needs to know a DOM backend exists.
- requirement: (MUST, testable) BECAUSE a DOM tree is a set of discrete, addressable elements rather
  than a single painted raster surface, this subclass MUST maintain an explicit STYLE/TRANSFORM STACK
  context object — `{fillColor, strokeColor, strokeWidth, opacity, shadow, transformMatrix, clipStack}`
  — pushed by `save()` and popped by `restore()`. Every subsequent geometry/text/image call MUST create
  its DOM element(s) styled and positioned from the CURRENT top-of-stack state at call time, never from
  a later or earlier state. `translate`/`rotate`/`scale` compose into the current transform matrix
  (never overwrite it); `clip_rect`/`clip_round_rect` push a CSS `overflow: hidden` / `clip-path`
  boundary that every element created before its matching `restore()` (or before a nested clip narrows
  further) MUST respect.
- element-map: (architect-synthesis — the primitive-to-DOM mapping this backend had to invent; no
  existing reference for it, unlike the SVG-element-to-primitive map `canvas_renderer.py` already
  documents in the OTHER direction) `fill_rect`/`fill_round_rect` -> a positioned `div`
  (`background-color`, `border-radius` for the rounded form); `stroke_rect`/`stroke_round_rect` -> a
  positioned `div` with `border` and no fill; `fill_circle`/`stroke_circle` -> a positioned `div` with
  `border-radius: 50%`; `fill_arc`/`stroke_arc`/`draw_path`/`fill_path` -> an inline SVG `<path>`
  (arbitrary beziers and arc sectors have no pure-CSS primitive; an inline `<svg>` fragment is the DOM-
  native escape hatch, exactly as `canvas_renderer.py` already uses SVG `path` data as its own
  interchange shape); `draw_line` -> an inline SVG `<line>` (a rotated 1px `div` cannot represent a
  non-integer-angle line without subpixel seams); `draw_text` -> a positioned `span`/`div` with `font`,
  `color`, and CSS text-alignment matching the `align` parameter; `draw_image` -> an `<img>` or a `div`
  with `background-image`, sized to the destination rect; `set_shadow`/`clear_shadow` -> CSS
  `box-shadow`/`filter: drop-shadow(...)`; `set_opacity` -> CSS `opacity` on the element's own style
  (composed with any inherited opacity from an ancestor, never overwriting it).
- measure-text-decision: (architect-synthesis, an implementation decision this spec makes explicit
  rather than leaving for the builder to invent) `measure_text` MUST be implemented via a synchronous
  DOM measurement against a hidden, off-screen probe element carrying the SAME `set_font` state
  (`getBoundingClientRect()` or `Range.getBoundingClientRect()` on a text node inside it) — never via an
  async API and never via a hardcoded metrics table, because the whole point of `measure_text` on every
  other backend is that it reflects the ACTUAL rendering font, and only a real DOM measurement does that
  in a browser.
- present-decision: `create_surface` MUST create the scene ROOT container element (an absolutely-
  positioned `div` sized to the requested width/height) that every subsequent element is appended under.
  `present()` MUST at minimum be a correct no-op-or-flush (DOM mutations from draw calls are already
  live in the tree the moment they run) and SHOULD batch same-frame mutations into one `DocumentFragment`
  append to avoid one reflow per draw call — the DOM-native equivalent of "flush drawing commands to the
  display". `present_partial` inherits the ABC's concrete full-`present()` default unless overridden to
  re-touch only the DOM subtree owning the damaged rect.
- negative-control: two `save()`/`restore()` pairs around two different `set_fill_color()` calls produce
  two elements with DIFFERENT fill colors, never both taking the color set after both saves (proving the
  stack, not a single mutable global, drives each element's style); a `clip_rect` followed by an element
  drawn OUTSIDE that rect, followed by `restore()`, is visibly clipped in the DOM (an `overflow:hidden`
  ancestor bounds it) rather than silently drawn full-size.
- verify-machine: a golden-fixture test draws a fixed sequence of primitive calls (mirroring the
  existing SVG/Skia backend parity fixtures) and asserts the resulting DOM tree's element count, tag
  types, and computed style values match the element-map table above; a save/restore/clip stress test
  asserts stack correctness per the negative-control.
- verify-human: a rendered fixture scene compared side-by-side against the SAME scene's Skia
  (`render_headless`) and SVG renders confirms visually equivalent layout, color, and text — not pixel-
  identical (DOM font rendering differs from Skia's), but recognizably the same design.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis — Fork C's "reuse the contract" applied concretely; the element map,
  the style/transform-stack model, and the measure-text/present decisions are this spec's own invention,
  flagged as such rather than attributed to David.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DOM]
- see-also: [CXC-R-DRAW-CONTRACT], [CXC-DOM-REALIZATION-SPLIT], [DX-NARROW-WAIST]

---

## 6. Control -> publish contract is reused, re-realized in the browser

### [CXC-DOM-CONTROL-PUBLISH-REUSE] MUST re-realize each existing control widget's publish contract identically in the browser; MUST NOT invent a new publish shape per DOM control

- requirement: (MUST, testable) for every widget type this backend realizes natively
  ([CXC-DOM-REALIZATION-SPLIT]'s control renderer registry), the DOM event that corresponds to that
  widget's existing Python interaction handler MUST produce the SAME publish call shape
  (`namespace`, `value`, `source`) the native widget already produces. Concretely: a `<button>` `click`
  event for an `emit_button`-realized control MUST publish the SAME `emits:` list of `{namespace,
  value}` pairs `on_tap()` already publishes, resolved against the SAME live-binding-substitution rule
  (`_resolve()`); a checkbox/toggle `change` event for a `theme_toggle`-shaped control publishes its
  bound namespace's new boolean; a radio-group `change` event publishes the selected index to
  `index_namespace`, matching `RadioButtonGroupWidget`'s `index_for()`/publish contract.
  This is a RE-REALIZATION, not a redesign: the builder MUST consult the corresponding Python widget
  module (`render/widgets/*.py`) as the reference publish contract for each control type, never invent a
  new payload shape independently.
- requirement: (MUST, testable) every publish this backend performs MUST travel over the WS connector's
  wire format verbatim ([CXC-WS-WIRE-FORMAT]) — the browser runtime constructs the SAME `BusMessage`
  JSON envelope shape the connector round-trips, never a simplified or DOM-specific payload shape.
- negative-control: two DOM-realized controls of the SAME widget TYPE with different `emits`/binding
  configuration produce DIFFERENT publish payloads on interaction (proving the payload is resolved from
  the control's OWN declared config, not a shared template); a control's publish is NEVER emitted before
  its triggering DOM event fires (no eager/prefetch publish).
- verify-machine: a scripted DOM interaction (dispatch a `click`/`change` event) against each v1-
  reused control type asserts the resulting outbound WS frame matches, byte-for-byte on
  `namespace`/`value`, what the equivalent headless Python widget test asserts for the same
  configuration and the same live-binding context.
- verify-human: a real browser click on a dashboard-style DOM control (e.g. a re-realized `emit_button`)
  is observed by a subscriber on the SAME bus (Python or another browser tab) — confirming the publish
  actually reaches a live peer over the real WS connector, not merely that a JS function was called
  ([CXC-WS-ACCEPTANCE]'s WS-V2/WS-V8 already cover the connector's own half of this path).
- status: proposed
- provenance: david-ratified-by-concurrence — re-tagged 2026-08-25 per [DEC-DOM-AUDIT-RESOLVED-2026-08-25]
  (audit finding ALTERED-2). This is the architect's Fork B proposal (line 26404): "the control->publish
  CONTRACT is REUSED (exists natively: emit_button/text_input/radio publish via
  RenderContext.publish()); the DOM backend RE-REALIZES it (a `<button>` click -> the same `emits:`
  publish; a `<input type=checkbox>` change -> the same value publish). The write travels browser->bus
  over the WS endpoint." David never spoke to this mechanic individually; his entire ratification of the
  whole of Fork B was the blanket "I concur, proceed" (line 26423). The node remains authoritative. The
  per-widget-type mapping detail (theme_toggle, radio_button_group's `index_for()`) is architect-synthesis,
  grounded in reading the cited widget modules.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DOM]
- see-also: [CXC-DOM-REALIZATION-SPLIT], [CXC-WS-WIRE-FORMAT], [CXC-DOM-PUBLISH-MODES]

---

## 7. Two publish modes — live-publish (existing) and form-buffered-atomic-submit (NEW)

### [CXC-DOM-PUBLISH-MODES] MUST select the publish mode by form-container membership: live-publish outside a FORM, form-buffered-atomic-submit inside one

- requirement: (MUST, testable) a control widget realized OUTSIDE any [CXC-DOM-FORM-PRIMITIVE]
  container MUST publish on every triggering interaction immediately — LIVE-PUBLISH, the existing
  behavior [CXC-DOM-CONTROL-PUBLISH-REUSE] already specifies, unchanged. This is the dashboard-control
  case: a toggle, a live-value slider, a command button.
- requirement: (MUST, testable) a control widget realized INSIDE a `[CXC-DOM-FORM-PRIMITIVE]` container
  MUST NOT publish on its own triggering interaction. Instead its changed value MUST be written into the
  enclosing form's LOCAL BUFFER only — FORM-BUFFERED-ATOMIC-SUBMIT, the NEW mode this spec introduces.
  No bus traffic MUST occur for a buffered field's edit until the form's own submit action fires
  ([CXC-DOM-FORM-PRIMITIVE]).
- requirement: (MUST, testable) which mode applies is determined STRUCTURALLY by DOM/scene containment
  (is this control's nearest ancestor widget a form primitive, yes or no) — never by a per-control
  authored flag that could disagree with its actual container, and never by widget TYPE (the identical
  `text` control type behaves live outside a form and buffered inside one).
- negative-control: moving an identical control from outside a form to inside one (same widget config,
  same binding) changes its behavior from immediate-publish to buffered-until-submit with NO other
  change to the control's own declaration — proving the mode is a property of placement, not of the
  control.
- verify-machine: a test scene with the SAME control type both inside and outside a form asserts:
  outside-form interaction produces an immediate outbound publish; inside-form interaction produces
  ZERO outbound publishes and a buffer-state change instead, until the form's submit fires.
- verify-human: none required beyond [CXC-DOM-ACCEPTANCE]'s form-submission glass gate, which already
  exercises the buffered path end-to-end.
- status: proposed
- provenance: david-ratified-by-concurrence — re-tagged 2026-08-25 per [DEC-DOM-AUDIT-RESOLVED-2026-08-25]
  (audit finding ALTERED-2). This is the architect's Fork B proposal (line 26404): "TWO publish modes,
  selected by form-container membership: live-publish (dashboard control, publishes on change — exists
  today) and form-buffered-atomic-submit (config editing — NEW)." David never spoke to this mechanic
  individually; his entire ratification of the whole of Fork B was the blanket "I concur, proceed" (line
  26423). The node remains authoritative. The structural-containment-not-a-flag determination rule is
  architect-synthesis, closing the ambiguity a builder would otherwise have to guess at.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DOM]
- see-also: [CXC-DOM-CONTROL-PUBLISH-REUSE], [CXC-DOM-FORM-PRIMITIVE]

---

## 8. The FORM primitive (NEW)

### [CXC-DOM-FORM-PRIMITIVE] MUST implement a NEW container widget that buffers child control values locally, validates on submit, and publishes the validated config atomically

- requirement: (MUST, testable) a NEW widget type, `form` (or an equivalently named container widget),
  MUST: (1) own a LOCAL BUFFER — a plain object keyed by each contained control's field name/namespace,
  initialized from each control's current bound value at mount; (2) update ONLY that buffer, never the
  bus, when a contained control fires its change event ([CXC-DOM-PUBLISH-MODES]); (3) on an explicit
  SUBMIT action (a submit button, or the form's own committed keyboard affordance — Enter, matching the
  existing `text_input.commit()` precedent), send the WHOLE buffered object to the server for validation
  ([CXC-DOM-SERVER-VALIDATION]); (4) publish the validated config to the bus ATOMICALLY — one bus write
  (or one coherent set of writes with no partially-applied intermediate state observable by another
  peer) — ONLY after the server confirms validation success; (5) on a validation failure, publish
  NOTHING, keep every field's edited (unpublished) value in the buffer, and surface the returned
  per-field errors ([CXC-DOM-SERVER-VALIDATION]) next to their fields.
- generalizes: this generalizes `TextInputWidget`'s existing single-field commit-buffer model (edit
  locally, commit on Return/blur, publish once) to MULTIPLE fields committed together as one unit — the
  same "buffer, then commit" shape, scaled from one namespace to a `{field: value}` object.
- requirement: (MUST, testable) a form MUST NOT allow a nested form (a form widget as a descendant of
  another form widget) in v1 — this is a stated boundary (see [CXC-DOM-OUT-OF-SCOPE] /
  [DD-DOM-EDITABLE-COLLECTIONS]), not a silent gap: a scene author nesting forms gets a loud parse/mount-
  time error naming the offending widget id, never a form whose submit semantics are ambiguous.
- negative-control: editing three fields in a form and never submitting produces ZERO bus publishes for
  any of the three, even after the form unmounts (an abandoned edit is discarded, not auto-committed);
  submitting a form with one invalid field produces ZERO bus publishes for ANY field in that form — a
  partially-valid submission never partially applies.
- verify-machine: a test form with three bound fields asserts: editing all three without submitting
  yields zero publishes; submitting with all three valid yields exactly one atomic publish sequence
  carrying all three values; submitting with one deliberately invalid field yields zero publishes and a
  non-empty per-field error surfaced on the SPECIFIC invalid field, with the other two fields' edited
  values still present in the buffer (not reverted, not lost).
- verify-human: an operator fills out a real config form in a real browser, submits, sees the config
  actually take effect on a live plugin (or, on a deliberate typo, sees the specific field's error
  inline) — the end-to-end proof that "validated config, applied atomically" is what actually happens,
  not merely what a unit test asserts.
- status: proposed
- provenance: david-ratified-by-concurrence — re-tagged 2026-08-25 per [DEC-DOM-AUDIT-RESOLVED-2026-08-25]
  (audit finding ALTERED-2). This is the architect's Fork B proposal (line 26404): "The FORM primitive
  (NEW): a container that buffers child controls' values locally, validates on submit, publishes the
  validated config atomically — generalizes text_input's existing commit-buffer model to multiple
  fields." David never spoke to this mechanic individually; his entire ratification of the whole of Fork
  B was the blanket "I concur, proceed" (line 26423). The node remains authoritative. The nested-form
  boundary and the atomicity failure-mode details are architect-synthesis.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DOM]
- see-also: [CXC-DOM-PUBLISH-MODES], [CXC-DOM-SERVER-VALIDATION], [CXC-DOM-CONTROL-SET-V1]

---

## 9. Server-side validation — reusing the loader, never re-implemented in JS

### [CXC-DOM-SERVER-VALIDATION] MUST validate a form submission server-side against the SAME pydantic `Config` evaluator the plugin loader already uses; MUST NOT implement a client-side JS re-derivation of validation logic

- requirement: (MUST, testable) on submit, the form's buffered `{field: value}` object MUST be sent to
  the server over the WS connection as the config payload for the target plugin/entity. The server MUST
  validate it by constructing `cls.Config(**payload)` — the IDENTICAL call sequence
  [CXC-CSL-CONFIG-VALIDATION] already specifies for the plugin loader, reused HERE rather than a second,
  independent validation code path. This backend MUST NOT ship any client-side JavaScript that
  re-implements field-type checking, enum membership, or any other rule a pydantic `Config` model
  encodes — the whole point (per [CXC-CS-RULE-CONFIG-UX]'s "validation is the real evaluator") is that a
  config that validates is a config that will actually run the same way, which is only true if there is
  ONE evaluator, not two that could drift.
- requirement: (MUST, testable) a validation failure MUST return the SAME `config:<field>: <message>`
  taxonomy [CXC-CSL-ERROR-TAXONOMY] already defines — one item per invalid field — over the WS
  connection back to the form, which MUST display each returned error next to the field it names (not
  a generic "invalid config" banner). This is the wire-level generalization of
  [CXC-CS-RULE-CONFIG-UX]'s "validation at config time runs the REAL evaluator" to the browser: the
  browser never re-derives the taxonomy, it displays what the server already computed.
- requirement: (SHOULD, testable) client-side JS SHOULD perform ONLY presentation-layer input
  constraints that have no bearing on config correctness (an `<input type="number">`'s browser-native
  numeric keypad, a `maxlength` on a text field) — never a rule whose failure would also be a pydantic
  `ValidationError`. A number field's browser-native min/max attribute MAY mirror a pydantic constraint
  for UX responsiveness, but the SERVER validation remains authoritative and MUST still run and MUST
  still be able to reject a value the browser-native constraint let through (a stale/incorrectly-mirrored
  client hint is a UX defect, never a correctness gap, because the server check is unconditional).
- negative-control: a value the client-side hint would have accepted but that the SERVER'S pydantic
  model rejects is STILL rejected end-to-end (proving the server call is unconditional, not skipped when
  the client hint passed); disabling JavaScript client-side constraints entirely (submitting a
  deliberately out-of-range raw value via a scripted WS frame) still produces the correct server-side
  rejection — proving no validation logic silently lives only in the browser.
- verify-machine: a test submits a form payload with one field failing a pydantic constraint the
  client-side hint would NOT have caught (a cross-field constraint, or a constraint the DOM control type
  cannot natively express) and asserts the server rejects it with the correct `config:<field>` reason,
  displayed on that field.
- verify-human: an operator deliberately submits an invalid config (e.g. a value the pydantic model
  rejects for a reason not visible from the form UI alone, like a cross-field constraint) and confirms
  the returned error is READABLE and NAMES the specific field — the same admin-UX-names-the-unmet-item
  discipline [CXC-CSL-STATUS-SURFACE] already requires for capability parks, applied here to config
  validation.
- loader-landed-reuse-tightened: (diamond-remediation, 2026-08-25) [SPEC:CXC-CS-LOADER] has now LANDED
  (`42d3285`, `composeserver_loader.py`), so "reuse [CXC-CSL-CONFIG-VALIDATION]" is no longer a forward
  reference to an unbuilt unit — it names real code. The WS<->DOM integration pass is wiring the reuse
  PROPERLY rather than by parallel re-derivation: it factors the loader's validation into a SHARED
  `validate_config(cls, payload)` function that BOTH the loader and this backend's own `validation.py`
  IMPORT — closing the "second independent validation path" the gap-analyzer flagged as finding P1. This
  node's MUST NOT-re-implement requirement above now has a concrete shared function to point at: this
  backend's server-side validation call is an IMPORT of that function, never a re-derivation of the same
  `cls.Config(**payload)` call sequence written a second time.
- status: proposed
- provenance: david-ruling — Fork B, the starred call: "Validation is SERVER-side, reusing the loader
  (david-ruling): the form submits the config over WS -> the server runs the REAL pydantic Config
  validation (the SAME [CXC-CSL-CONFIG-VALIDATION] the loader does), returns per-field errors (already
  emitted as config:<field>: <msg>), the form displays them. NO client-side JS re-implementation of
  pydantic validation." The presentation-layer-hints-only SHOULD and its negative controls are
  architect-synthesis, closing the "how far can client-side JS go" ambiguity explicitly.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DOM]
- see-also: [CXC-CSL-CONFIG-VALIDATION], [CXC-CSL-ERROR-TAXONOMY], [CXC-CS-RULE-CONFIG-UX], [CXC-DOM-FORM-PRIMITIVE]

---

## 10. Two-way value binding

### [CXC-DOM-TWO-WAY-BINDING] MUST subscribe->display via `AttrBinding` AND write on event, for both publish modes

- requirement: (MUST, testable) every DOM-realized control MUST bind its displayed value via the SAME
  `AttrBinding` semantics ([CXC-BIND-ATTR]) every other backend already honors: the runtime subscribes
  the control's bound namespace, and on a bus change coerces the raw value via the SAME coercion
  contract `coerce_bound_value` specifies (ported to JS, conformance-matched per
  [CXC-DOM-REUSE-REIMPLEMENT-RUNTIME]) and sets it as the DOM element's displayed value — WITHIN ONE
  FRAME of the bus event, mirroring [CXC-BIND-C1]'s existing latency bound.
  This is the READ half.
- requirement: (MUST, testable) the WRITE half is [CXC-DOM-PUBLISH-MODES]'s existing split: a live-
  publish control writes to the bus on its own triggering event; a form-buffered control writes to the
  form's local buffer on its own triggering event and to the bus only on form submit
  ([CXC-DOM-FORM-PRIMITIVE]). Either way, a control's OWN publish (or buffer-write) of a value it just
  wrote MUST NOT be immediately re-applied to itself as an incoming bind update in a way that fights the
  user's in-progress edit — the SAME one-shot echo-suppression precedent `TextInputWidget` already
  establishes for its `clear_on_commit` case.
- requirement: (MUST, testable) a form-buffered control's DISPLAYED value, while the form is open and
  unsubmitted, MUST reflect the user's own uncommitted edit (from the local buffer), NOT a concurrent
  live bus update to that same namespace from another peer — the buffer is the authoritative display
  source for a field currently being edited in a form, exactly as `TextInputWidget` already refuses a
  bus echo to stomp an in-progress edit.
- negative-control: a bus-side change to a namespace bound by a form-buffered field that is CURRENTLY
  being edited does NOT overwrite the user's uncommitted keystrokes; the SAME bus-side change to the
  SAME namespace bound by a LIVE (non-form) control DOES update that control's display immediately.
- verify-machine: a scripted bus publish to a namespace bound by both a live control and a form-buffered
  (open, edited) control asserts the live control's display updates and the form-buffered control's
  display does NOT, within one frame of the publish.
- verify-human: a real two-tab scenario — one tab shows a live dashboard control, another has a config
  form open with a field bound to the same namespace mid-edit — confirms the live tab updates instantly
  while the editing tab's in-progress value is undisturbed until submit or cancel.
- status: proposed
- provenance: david-ratified-by-concurrence — re-tagged 2026-08-25 per [DEC-DOM-AUDIT-RESOLVED-2026-08-25]
  (audit finding ALTERED-2). This is the architect's Fork B proposal (line 26404): "Two-way value
  binding: each control subscribes->displays the current value (AttrBinding) AND writes on event (to
  the form buffer, or straight to the bus for a live control)." David never spoke to this mechanic
  individually; his entire ratification of the whole of Fork B was the blanket "I concur, proceed" (line
  26423). The node remains authoritative. The echo-suppression and buffer-precedence rules are
  architect-synthesis, extending the existing `TextInputWidget` precedent to the form-buffered case
  explicitly.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DOM]
- see-also: [CXC-DOM-PUBLISH-MODES], [CXC-DOM-FORM-PRIMITIVE], [CXC-DOM-REUSE-REIMPLEMENT-RUNTIME]

---

## 11. v1 control set

### [CXC-DOM-CONTROL-SET-V1] MUST support exactly this field-control vocabulary for a form; MUST NOT attempt editable nested-collection controls in v1

- requirement: (MUST, testable) the v1 form-field control vocabulary is exactly: text (native
  `<input type="text">`), number (native `<input type="number">`), checkbox (native
  `<input type="checkbox">`), select — for an enum/`Literal` pydantic field (native `<select>`),
  textarea (native `<textarea>`), date/time (native `<input type="date"|"time"|"datetime-local">`), a
  READ-ONLY table (a real `<table>`, for displaying a list/rows of structured data), and a READ-ONLY
  tree (an ARIA `role="tree"` structure, for namespace/hierarchy review — David: "very useful for
  namespace review"). Every one of these uses the corresponding native HTML element or ARIA pattern,
  never a custom-drawn substitute, per [CXC-DOM-SCOPE-SPLIT].
- requirement: (MUST, testable) EDITABLE nested-collection controls — an editable table for a
  `list[Model]` pydantic field, an editable/mutable tree — are explicitly OUT OF SCOPE for v1 and MUST
  NOT be attempted. This is a stated deferral ([DD-DOM-EDITABLE-COLLECTIONS]), not a silent gap: a config
  field whose shape needs one MUST fall back to the ALREADY-RATIFIED raw-mode DSL path
  ([CXC-CS-RULE-CONFIG-UX]'s two-mode rule config UX — templates + hand-typed validated YAML) rather
  than this backend inventing a partial/broken structured editor for it.
  the-read-only-table-and-tree-are-not-that-deferral: the READ-ONLY table/tree in this v1 set are
  DISPLAY-ONLY (no mutation path back to the bus) and therefore are NOT the deferred editable-collection
  case — a builder MUST NOT read "table and tree are v1" as license to also ship editing on them.
- verify-machine: a fixture pydantic `Config` model covering every v1 field type (str, int/float, bool,
  a `Literal`/enum field, a long-text field, a date field, a `list[dict]` field for read-only table
  display, a nested-dict field for read-only tree display) round-trips through the form's mount ->
  buffer -> display path with the correct native element per field, and any field whose pydantic type
  is `list[SomeModel]` (an EDITABLE nested collection) is rendered via the raw-mode DSL fallback path,
  never via an attempted structured editor.
- verify-human: David or the render-engineer reviews a real config form generated for a real plugin's
  pydantic schema and confirms every field type present renders with the correct native control and
  that a genuinely nested-collection field correctly falls back to raw-mode rather than rendering a
  broken partial editor.
- status: proposed
- q2-confirmed-2026-08-25: David confirmed ([DEC-DOM-AUDIT-RESOLVED-2026-08-25], resolving the audit's
  Q2) that READ-ONLY table (not just tree) is the correct v1 line — an editable table falls to the
  ratified raw-mode DSL fallback ([DD-DOM-EDITABLE-COLLECTIONS]) exactly as an editable tree already
  does.
- provenance: david-ratified-by-concurrence — re-tagged 2026-08-25 per [DEC-DOM-AUDIT-RESOLVED-2026-08-25]
  (audit finding ALTERED-1). The eight-item vocabulary (text, number, checkbox, select (enum/Literal),
  textarea, date/time (native <input type=...>), read-only table, read-only tree) and the read-only/
  editable split are the ARCHITECT'S proposed construction (line 26419), which David RATIFIED BY
  CONCURRENCE ("I concur, proceed," line 26423) rather than enumerated himself. David's own words were a
  conditional lean plus one named control: "I lean to implementing all the heavier ones alongside the
  simpler ones if it's 'just more work' vs a different class of design challenge. Tree in particular can
  be very useful for reviewing namespaces" (line 26411) — cited directly in the requirement above, not
  attributed to the full list. The node remains authoritative — David did concur — but the quoted
  vocabulary is the architect's proposal, not David's verbatim enumeration. The "read-only is not the
  deferral" clarification remains architect-synthesis, closing a plausible misreading.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DOM]
- see-also: [CXC-DOM-FORM-PRIMITIVE], [CXC-CS-RULE-CONFIG-UX], [DD-DOM-EDITABLE-COLLECTIONS]

