## 12. The config-UI generator is a CONSUMER of this spec, not part of it

### [CXC-DOM-CONFIG-UI-CONSUMER] The pydantic-schema -> form mapping is OUT of scope here; this spec defines only the control layer and form primitive it consumes

- claim: a separate unit (not designed by this spec) maps a plugin's pydantic `Config` schema to a
  concrete form built from [CXC-DOM-CONTROL-SET-V1]'s vocabulary — field name -> control type, pydantic
  constraints -> native HTML constraint attributes where expressible, a `Literal`/enum field -> a
  `<select>`'s option list. That generator is a CONSUMER of the control layer and form primitive this
  spec defines; it does not need its own bespoke publish/validation mechanics because it composes
  entirely from what [CXC-DOM-FORM-PRIMITIVE] and [CXC-DOM-SERVER-VALIDATION] already provide.
- why-separate: keeping the generator as its own unit means this spec's control layer stays useful for
  ANY form (an operator hand-authoring a scene with specific fields), not only schema-generated ones —
  the narrow-waist discipline applied to authoring surfaces, not just runtime backends.
- status: proposed
- provenance: david-ratified-by-concurrence — re-tagged 2026-08-25 per [DEC-DOM-AUDIT-RESOLVED-2026-08-25]
  (audit finding ALTERED-2). This is the architect's Fork B proposal (line 26404): "The config-UI
  (pydantic-schema -> form) is a CONSUMER of this spec, a separate unit — this spec defines the control
  layer + form primitive; the config-UI generator maps a plugin's pydantic schema to a form of these
  controls." David never spoke to this individually; his entire ratification of the whole of Fork B was
  the blanket "I concur, proceed" (line 26423). The node remains authoritative.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DOM]
- see-also: [CXC-DOM-CONTROL-SET-V1], [CXC-DOM-FORM-PRIMITIVE], [DX-NARROW-WAIST]

---

## 13. Reuse the contract, reimplement the runtime — the third reactive tier

### [CXC-DOM-REUSE-REIMPLEMENT-RUNTIME] MUST reuse the Canvas ABC, scene format, binding-DSL grammar, control-publish vocabulary, and server validation BY REFERENCE; MUST reimplement the browser runtime in JS, conformance-matched to the Python golden reference

- requirement: (MUST, testable) this backend MUST NOT fork or duplicate: the `Canvas` ABC's method
  signatures and semantics ([CXC-R-DRAW-CONTRACT] — [CXC-DOM-CANVAS-SUBCLASS] implements it, never
  redefines it); the `.compose.yaml` scene document format (widget tree, `design.*` keys); the binding-
  DSL grammar (`AttrBinding`'s `{namespace, when, invert, map, default, expr}` shape, and the sandboxed
  expression language `[SPEC:CXC-DSL]` defines); the control-publish vocabulary (each widget type's
  publish payload shape, [CXC-DOM-CONTROL-PUBLISH-REUSE]); or server-side validation logic
  ([CXC-DOM-SERVER-VALIDATION] — never re-implemented client-side).
- requirement: (MUST, testable) this backend MUST reimplement, in JavaScript, its own RUNTIME: parsing a
  scene document, building the DOM tree from it, wiring bindings (subscribe on mount, apply on bus
  change), patching the DOM reactively on subsequent bus events, and publishing on control events. This
  is the THIRD reactive tier the platform now carries — Python/Skia (desktop/server), C++/CYD
  (embedded), and JS/DOM (browser) — each independently implementing the SAME declarative contract
  against its own platform's native capabilities, exactly as the CYD C++ tier already does relative to
  the Python tier ([CXC-PRIM-TIER-PARITY]).
- requirement: (MUST, testable) the JS binding-expression evaluator (whatever mechanism realizes
  [SPEC:CXC-DSL]'s sandboxed expressions in the browser) MUST conformance-match the Python golden
  reference evaluator on every case the existing Python DSL test corpus already covers — the SAME
  one-measurement-path / cross-tier-parity discipline the CYD/Skia glyph and justification corpora
  already apply ([CXC-TM1637-FORMAT-BACKEND]'s "the oracle is the raster realization, not a round trip"
  precedent, applied here to the DSL instead of seven-segment glyphs). A JS evaluator that merely LOOKS
  equivalent by inspection, without a corpus proving it against the same cases the Python evaluator is
  proven against, does not satisfy this requirement.
- mechanism-choice-resolved: (diamond-remediation, 2026-08-25) HOW that conformance is achieved —
  transpiling the Python-authored expression grammar into JS at build/publish time, versus an
  independent JS reimplementation gated by a shared conformance corpus — was the open fork
  [DD-DOM-DSL-EVAL-REUSE]; it is now RESOLVED by implementation: an independent, hand-written JS
  evaluator gated by the shared `dsl_corpus.json` conformance corpus (also consumed by the CH32/CYD
  tier), measured 234/234 bit-exact IEEE-754 against the Python golden reference. See
  [DD-DOM-DSL-EVAL-REUSE] for the full resolution record.
- negative-control: a scene that runs correctly against the Python headless runtime and is then served
  to this DOM backend produces IDENTICAL widget-tree structure and IDENTICAL resolved binding values for
  every namespace both runtimes subscribe — proving "reuse the contract" is not merely aspirational
  but produces the same graph both tiers agree on.
- verify-machine: the SAME `.compose.yaml` fixture parsed by both the Python parser and the JS runtime
  produces structurally equivalent widget trees (id, type, declared bindings) — a cross-tier parity
  test, mirroring the existing Skia/SVG/CYD parity fixtures; the shared DSL conformance corpus
  (`dsl_corpus.json`, per [DD-DOM-DSL-EVAL-REUSE]'s resolution) passes identically against both the
  Python and JS evaluators — measured 234/234.
- verify-human: none required beyond what other nodes' glass gates already cover — this node's own
  failure surface (a scene that parses differently, or a DSL expression that evaluates differently,
  cross-tier) is fully machine-observable via the parity/conformance corpora.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis — Fork C (CYD precedent): "REUSE by reference: the Canvas 30-method
  ABC, the .compose.yaml scene format, the binding-DSL grammar, the control-publish vocabulary, the
  server-side validation. REIMPLEMENT in JS: the browser runtime... the third reactive tier after
  Python/Skia and C++/CYD. The JS binding-expression evaluator MUST conformance-match the Python golden
  reference... [DD-DOM-DSL-EVAL-REUSE] names the open mechanism choice... render-engineer's call under
  the conformance constraint." Quoted from the dispatch verbatim per the design session's own framing of
  this as architect-synthesis, not David's word.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DOM]
- see-also: [CXC-R-DRAW-CONTRACT], [CXC-PRIM-TIER-PARITY], [CXC-TM1637-FORMAT-BACKEND], [DD-DOM-DSL-EVAL-REUSE]

---

## 14. Transport — rides the WS endpoint; the SSE bridge coexists read-only

### [CXC-DOM-TRANSPORT] MUST use the WS connector as this backend's bidirectional transport; the existing SSE bridge remains legacy read-only and is NOT extended for writes

- requirement: (MUST, testable) this backend's browser runtime MUST open its bus connection over the
  WS connector already fully specified at [SPEC:CXC-WS] — the SAME `BusMessage.to_json()`/`from_json()`
  envelope ([CXC-WS-WIRE-FORMAT]), the SAME subscription model ([CXC-WS-SUBSCRIPTION]), the SAME
  retained-state replay on subscribe ([CXC-WS-RETAINED-REPLAY]). This backend does not open a second,
  competing connection mechanism.
- requirement: (MUST, testable) the EXISTING `WebBusModule`/SSE bridge (`composeio/web.py`,
  `static/bus-events.js`) MUST NOT be extended with a write/publish path for this backend's controls.
  It remains what it already is — a read-only, one-way bus-to-browser push for legacy pages — and this
  backend's controls (which need to WRITE) are out of its scope by construction, not by an added
  restriction.
- why-ws-not-sse: SSE is server-to-client only by protocol; a control's publish (browser-to-bus) has no
  SSE-shaped path at all, and inventing an HTTP POST side-channel just for writes would fork this
  backend's wire format away from [CXC-WS-WIRE-FORMAT]'s already-specified, already-symmetric envelope
  for no benefit.
- negative-control: this backend's own runtime code contains no reference to `/events` (the SSE route)
  or to `EventSource` — it is built entirely against the WS connector's own client-side API surface.
- verify-machine: a static/dependency scan of this backend's JS source asserts no `EventSource`
  reference and no fetch/POST call standing in for a publish (every publish goes over the open WS
  connection); a live test opens a WS connection via [CXC-WS-CONNECTOR-INTERFACE] and confirms this
  backend's runtime binds/publishes over it successfully.
- verify-human: none required beyond [CXC-WS-ACCEPTANCE]'s own glass gates for the connector, which this
  backend inherits by using the same transport.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis, restating the design session's explicit transport call ("Transport:
  WS (bidirectional); the SSE bridge coexists for legacy read-only") as a queryable node.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DOM]
- see-also: [SPEC:CXC-WS], [CXC-WS-WIRE-FORMAT], [CXC-WS-SUBSCRIPTION]

---

## 15. Explicit out-of-scope — cited, not specified

### [CXC-DOM-OUT-OF-SCOPE] The units this spec deliberately does not design

- the-canvaskit-backend: the rich-graphics browser backend (gauges, data viz, the colonial/BSG UX) is
  its own, DEFERRED unit ([CXC-DOM-SCOPE-SPLIT]). This spec's obligation to it is narrow: leave the
  hybrid-composition seam ([CXC-DOM-HYBRID-COMPOSITION]) unforeclosed so a canvas island can mount
  beside DOM chrome once it exists.
- the-config-ui-generator: the pydantic-schema -> form mapping is a CONSUMER of this spec, not designed
  here ([CXC-DOM-CONFIG-UI-CONSUMER]).
- the-ws-connector-transport: the WS transport itself — connection lifecycle, tenant boundary, entry
  modes, auth position — is fully specified at [SPEC:CXC-WS] and only cited here as this backend's wire
  ([CXC-DOM-TRANSPORT]); this spec does not respecify any of it.
- editable-nested-collections: deferred to [DD-DOM-EDITABLE-COLLECTIONS], covered meanwhile by the
  ratified raw-mode DSL fallback.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis, restating the session's explicit scope boundaries as a queryable node
  rather than leaving them only in frontmatter prose.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DOM]
- see-also: [CXC-DOM-SCOPE-SPLIT], [CXC-DOM-CONFIG-UI-CONSUMER], [SPEC:CXC-WS], [DD-DOM-EDITABLE-COLLECTIONS]

