## 19. Open forks — flagged for the render-engineer's / architect's / David's ratify

### [DD-DOM-EDITABLE-COLLECTIONS] DEFERRED (settled) — editable nested-collection controls are a genuinely different design class, out of v1

- question: how should an editable table for a `list[Model]` pydantic field, or an editable/mutable
  tree, be designed — recursive form nesting, an inline row-editor, a modal-per-item pattern?
- why-deferred-not-open: this is not an unresolved ambiguity blocking v1 — David explicitly ruled it OUT
  of v1 because it is "a genuinely different design class" (recursive/nested forms), and the ALREADY-
  RATIFIED raw-mode DSL fallback ([CXC-CS-RULE-CONFIG-UX]) covers any config field this shape is needed
  for in the meantime, so nothing is blocked waiting on this fork.
- candidates: (not a recommendation, offered so the fork is concrete when it is picked up) (a) recursive
  form nesting with a relaxed version of [CXC-DOM-FORM-PRIMITIVE]'s no-nested-forms boundary, one
  sub-form per collection item; (b) an inline row-editor directly inside a table, each row its own
  small buffered unit; (c) a modal/drawer-per-item pattern, editing one collection element at a time
  against the SAME form-buffer-then-submit mechanics this spec already defines, just scoped to one item.
- decision: OPEN, deferred — not yet decided, and not scheduled against v1.
- rationale: see `why-deferred-not-open` above.
- alternatives: see `candidates` above.
- invalidating_assumption: once a real config schema is encountered in production that genuinely needs
  this (a plugin whose config legitimately contains a list of sub-models an operator must edit through
  the structured UI rather than raw-mode), the deferral needs revisiting — the raw-mode fallback is a
  legitimate v1 answer, not a permanent one.
- status: deferred
- status-detail: deferred — settled as OUT of v1; not an open ambiguity requiring an immediate ratify
- provenance: david-ruling — Fork B: "DEFER (a genuinely different design class — recursive/nested
  forms) the EDITABLE nested-collection controls... the ratified raw-mode fallback... covers any config
  too complex for the structured form meanwhile, so nothing is blocked." The candidate list is this
  spec-writer's own addition, offered to make the eventual fork concrete, not a recommendation.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DOM]
- see-also: [CXC-DOM-CONTROL-SET-V1], [CXC-CS-RULE-CONFIG-UX], [CXC-DOM-FORM-PRIMITIVE]

### [DD-DOM-TREE-VIRTUALIZATION] DEFERRED (settled) — tree windowing is a later scale concern, not a v1 blocker

- question: at what namespace/hierarchy count does the read-only tree control ([CXC-DOM-CONTROL-SET-V1])
  need virtualization/windowing (rendering only the visible subset of a large tree) rather than the
  naive full-DOM-tree rendering v1 ships with?
- why-deferred-not-open: v1's non-virtualized tree handles the hundreds of real namespaces this platform
  currently has; this is a forward-looking scale note, not a v1 requirement gap.
- decision: not decided; explicitly not scheduled against v1.
- rationale: see `why-deferred-not-open` above.
- alternatives: not enumerated here — a future windowing implementation (e.g. only mounting DOM nodes
  for the currently-scrolled-into-view subtree) is a performance optimization layered onto the SAME
  read-only tree control, not a new control type.
- invalidating_assumption: if a real deployment's namespace count grows past what naive full-DOM
  rendering can handle responsively (measured, not guessed), this deferral needs revisiting.
- status: deferred
- status-detail: deferred — noted for later, not a v1 blocker
- provenance: architect-synthesis — re-tagged 2026-08-25 per [DEC-DOM-AUDIT-RESOLVED-2026-08-25] (audit
  finding INVENTED-2). The virtualization/windowing caveat is the architect's own parenthetical (line
  26419: "at thousands of nodes you'd eventually want virtualization/windowing... a later optimization,
  not a v1 blocker"), not David's word. David's only statement about tree was "Tree in particular can be
  very useful for reviewing namespaces" (line 26411), which says nothing about scale or virtualization.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DOM]
- see-also: [CXC-DOM-CONTROL-SET-V1]

### [DD-DOM-DSL-EVAL-REUSE] RESOLVED — by implementation: candidate (b), an independent JS evaluator gated by the shared conformance corpus

- question: [CXC-DOM-REUSE-REIMPLEMENT-RUNTIME] specifies that the JS evaluator MUST conformance-match
  the Python golden reference — it does not specify HOW: transpile the Python-authored expression
  grammar into JS at build/publish time (one grammar, two emitted evaluators), versus an independent
  hand-written JS reimplementation gated by a shared conformance corpus (two grammars, one shared proof
  obligation).
- why-left-open-originally: this was an implementation-mechanism choice under a FIXED constraint
  (conformance), not a design-intent ambiguity — [CXC-DOM-REUSE-REIMPLEMENT-RUNTIME]'s requirement was
  fully testable and implementable regardless of which mechanism was chosen, so the choice did not block
  authoring this spec; it was left to the render-engineer's call.
- candidates: (a) transpile — parse the Python DSL grammar once, emit equivalent JS at scene-publish
  time or build time; guarantees identity by construction but couples the JS runtime to a build step
  that must run whenever the grammar changes. (b) independent JS reimplementation — hand-written JS
  evaluator gated by running the SAME conformance corpus [CXC-TM1637-FORMAT-BACKEND]'s glyph-corpus
  precedent already demonstrates for a different tier pair; cheaper to keep the JS runtime dependency-
  free but carries ongoing drift risk the corpus must keep catching.
- decision: RESOLVED — candidate (b): an INDEPENDENT, hand-written JS evaluator, gated by the SHARED
  conformance corpus (`dsl_corpus.json`), the SAME corpus the CH32/CYD tier already consumes for its own
  cross-tier parity proof. Measured: 234/234 cases bit-exact IEEE-754 against the Python golden
  reference. This is the one-measurement-path discipline [CXC-DOM-REUSE-REIMPLEMENT-RUNTIME] requires,
  genuinely satisfied per the gap-analyzer — one shared corpus, one shared proof obligation, consumed by
  every tier that needs conformance, not a per-tier ad hoc check.
- rationale: candidate (b) keeps the JS runtime dependency-free (no build-time transpile step coupling
  every DSL grammar change to a JS-emission pass) while still closing the drift risk the transpile
  alternative would have avoided by construction — the shared conformance corpus is the mechanism that
  closes it instead, and it already does double duty proving the CH32/CYD tier, so extending it to the
  JS tier is REUSE of an already-proven discipline, not a new one invented for this backend alone.
- alternatives: see `candidates` above — (a) transpile was not chosen; the corpus-gated independent
  reimplementation was judged the better fit given the corpus already existed and already served a
  second tier.
- invalidating_assumption: once the DSL grammar itself is revised ([SPEC:CXC-DSL]), the JS evaluator
  needs the conformance corpus extended to cover the new constructs before they can be trusted in the
  browser — the chosen mechanism (independent reimplementation) carries this ongoing obligation
  explicitly, as `why-left-open-originally`'s tradeoff analysis anticipated.
- status: decided
- status-detail: resolved by implementation — 234/234 bit-exact IEEE-754 against the Python golden
  reference via `dsl_corpus.json`, the shared corpus also consumed by the CH32/CYD tier
- provenance: architect-synthesis — the render-engineer's call under this node's fixed conformance
  constraint, per the gap-analyzer's endorsement of the measured 234/234 result. The fork was originally
  named `architect-proposed` in the design session ("[DD-DOM-DSL-EVAL-REUSE] names the open mechanism
  choice (share/transpile the grammar vs. conformance-gated JS reimpl), render-engineer's call under the
  conformance constraint"); the resolution recorded here is the render-engineer's actual implementation
  choice, confirmed by measurement, not a further David ratify — David never spoke to the mechanism
  choice, only to the fixed constraint it operates under.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DOM]
- see-also: [CXC-DOM-REUSE-REIMPLEMENT-RUNTIME], [CXC-TM1637-FORMAT-BACKEND]

